= Introduction <sec:intro>

Locks are among the oldest and most widely deployed synchronization primitives. Embedded key-value stores, database engines, file systems, and the operating system itself rely on them to protect the state that every request must eventually touch @redb @upscaledb. On a modern multicore these locks are rarely idle. Under steady load a contended lock is always held or about to be handed over, and at every hand-off it decides whose code runs next on the shared state. A contended lock is therefore more than a correctness device; it is a _scheduler_ of critical-section time. Yet locks have rarely been judged as schedulers. They have been judged by throughput, by scalability, and by whether each waiting thread eventually gets its turn.

A turn, however, is not a unit of service. Consider two threads, $T_0$ and $T_1$, that repeatedly write to an embedded database through its single-writer lock. $T_0$ commits one record per transaction; $T_1$ commits sixty-four. A FIFO lock such as a ticket lock or MCS @mellorcrummey1991mcs faithfully alternates between them, so each thread acquires the lock equally often. Yet $T_1$ holds the lock many times longer on every turn and ends up with most of the time the lock is held. We call this problem _service conflation_: a lock's acquisition order is mistaken for its allocation of service. When conflation arises, the length of a request's critical section, rather than any policy, decides how much of the shared resource that request consumes. The effect is visible in practice. When 1-record and 64-record writers share redb's write path, MCS achieves a Jain fairness index @jain1984fairness of only 0.85--0.86 over time spent in the critical section, and redb's native mutex is worse still. By the usual definition, both locks are fair.

The problem is not new, and two lines of work each solve half of it. _Scheduler-Cooperative Locks_ @patel2020scl recognized that lock usage, not acquisition, determines what share each thread receives. u-SCL tracks each thread's lock usage, penalizes threads that exceed their share, and grants each thread a _lock slice_, a window during which only the slice owner may acquire the lock. This restores proportional allocation, but consider its fairness target, _lock opportunity_:
$ "LOT"(i) = sum "CS"(i) + sum "Lock Idle Time". $
Idle time counts as opportunity, so idleness is legitimate by construction. A slice owner that leaves the critical section to run its own non-critical code keeps the lock reserved while others wait; u-SCL is, by design, _not work-conserving_. Enforcing slices also puts waiters to sleep, tying the lock's behaviour to the operating system's wake-up latency and power management.

_Delegation_ locks take the opposite approach: flat combining @hendler2010fc, CC-Synch @fatourou2012ccsynch, RCL @lozi2012rcl, and ffwd @roghanchi2017ffwd. Threads publish their critical sections as requests, and a single _combiner_ executes all pending requests on their behalf. Delegation keeps the protected data in one core's cache and never lets the lock sit idle while work is pending; it is the fastest known way to run a contended critical section. But delegation locks serve requests in arrival or scan order, and so inherit exactly the service conflation of FIFO locks. On redb, flat combining reaches a service fairness of only 0.83--0.85.

Existing designs thus force a choice between fairness in service and efficiency in execution. We observe that the choice is unnecessary, because delegation already contains the mechanism fairness needs. The combiner _is_ a scheduler. It sits exactly where the next critical section is chosen, it sees every pending request at once, and, because it executes each request itself, it can measure each one's cost precisely for the price of two timestamp reads. Fairness therefore need not be enforced _around_ the lock with slices, penalties, and sleeping; it can be expressed _inside_ the lock, as the order in which the combiner serves its queue.

Building on this observation, we introduce _service-fair delegation_ and its implementation, FC-PQ. FC-PQ combines three techniques: _service accounting_, in which the thread that executes a request measures its critical-section time and charges it to the thread that submitted it; _usage-ordered combining_, in which the combiner draws pending requests from a priority queue ordered by accumulated service, in the spirit of virtual-time fair queueing @demers1989wfq; and a _fast path_ that lets an uncontended thread execute directly, so fairness costs nothing when there is no one to be fair to. Unlike slice-based locks, FC-PQ never reserves the lock for an absent thread. Whenever any request is pending, the combiner runs one.

We evaluate FC-PQ inside two real databases rather than synthetic loops. In redb, FC-PQ replaces the single-writer admission, so each critical section is a complete write transaction including commit. In UpScaleDB, it replaces the global environment mutex that serializes finds and inserts. Evaluating fairness honestly required two methodological changes. First, we measure service fairness as Jain's index over critical-section time charged to the _requesting_ thread, whichever thread executed the request. Second, we fix the CPU clock. Under the stock frequency governor, u-SCL's sleeping waiters ran at about 1 GHz while spinning locks ran at turbo frequency, which made FC-PQ appear two to three times faster than u-SCL. With every core pinned at 3.0 GHz, that advantage disappears.

With the clock fixed, our redb results show the following. FC-PQ achieves a service fairness of 0.94--0.995, against 0.83--0.86 for flat combining and MCS, approaching u-SCL's 1.00. It commits 1.17--1.34$times$ the transactions per second of MCS: the combiner keeps the database's pages in its cache, incurring 10--18 cross-core cache transfers per transaction against MCS's 93--126. The gain is not free. Because FC-PQ favours short writers, its record throughput falls to 0.72--0.96$times$ that of MCS. And because its waiters spin, it spends about four times the CPU time of u-SCL at equal throughput with eight writers. Against u-SCL itself, FC-PQ reaches parity rather than superiority, both on redb and in the UpScaleDB evidence available so far. redb's writers submit back to back, leaving no non-critical time, which is precisely the case in which lock slices never waste the lock. We therefore state, as a falsifiable prediction, that FC-PQ's advantage over slice-based locks grows as $min(N, (c + n) / c)$, where $N$ is the number of competing threads, $c$ the critical-section time, and $n$ the non-critical time between requests.

This paper makes the following contributions:

- We identify _service conflation_, in which acquisition-fair locks, including existing delegation locks, grant very different amounts of service to different requesters, and we demonstrate it in a production embedded database.
- We show that the combiner of a delegation lock is a natural place to enforce service fairness, and we design FC-PQ, a service-fair, work-conserving delegation lock with a fast path proven sound for the uncontended case.
- We develop a methodology for measuring service fairness in real systems: service charged to the requester, correctness gates inside the database, and control of the CPU clock. We show that without clock control, comparisons between spinning and sleeping locks can be dominated by power management rather than lock design.
- We evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB against the native mutex, MCS, flat combining, and u-SCL, reporting where it helps, where it only matches the state of the art, and where our evidence does not yet reach.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 describes service conflation and shows that existing locks, delegation included, do not ensure service fairness. Section 3 presents the design and implementation of FC-PQ. Section 4 describes our methodology, and Sections 5 and 6 evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB. We discuss limitations in Section 7 and related work in Section 8, and conclude in Section 9.
